- y = 6$\n\nUse the diagram below to determine this system's solution $(x,y)$.\n\n![](images/6f/6f0b7960b9b4c98250741b009da2ad19c1123c5b7adf9cd5072430fb9a879dc9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138083 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cbf28a0c-c34d-5855-b72e-4225a11710c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138083 #edb/ref 1074 "The solution of the system corresponds to the point of intersection of the lines.\n\n![](images/5c/5c1c69adc880ea7cfdb5a4e0a10abd6032d2eedf0386923b94df9b7c173edd6d.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(4,2)$. Therefore, the solution to the given system is $(4,2)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138084 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138084 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/09/0915c99015922e27855d92ebb13756da634a8f10dd8fe3abbc016590f06e1077.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the number of liters of water in pool $A$ and pool $B$ as a function of time, measured in days. How many days will it take for both pools to contain the same amount of water?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138084 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86771ced-afc0-5061-a532-bc2345a52ea8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138084 #edb/ref 1074 "The pools have the same amount of water when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/cf/cf7a9b8bdccbc66086c473813523ed893ee192830965300a8c4a0702ad065fc3.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(5,400)$. So, both pools have the same amount ($400\\,\\textrm{L}$) of water at $t=5$ days." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Solution to a System From Its Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138428 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0f80bc47-7b56-5357-b4e7-718f4ddfa41b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186139116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1039 "Word Problems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138429 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4154d81b-c94a-57f7-80fa-0daa341505e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1045 "Introduction to Systems of Linear Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186139350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138586 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0a5bdaa9-fe87-516a-8c94-9bc96565f389" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139350 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139350 #edb/ref 1061 196434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139350 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "331a78d4-5c29-520f-a140-cfab1b9cf26b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139350 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139351 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139351 #edb/ref 1061 196435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139351 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bbbe689a-fd82-54c6-b18a-876c6124c4f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139351 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139352 #edb/ref 1061 196439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139352 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a86bd4f-c706-59ab-986e-311feb0009df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139352 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139667 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to know the values of $x$ and $y$ that satisfy *both* of the equations\n\n$$\n{x - y =-4; 6x - y = 6\n$$\n\nWe call this a **system of linear equations**. The equations of the system define two straight lines, so we can visualize the system by plotting both lines on the same graph.\n\n![](images/ee/ee391c2910264abc2962d0c5ec3d971eb7b0b818b5bd59af33fc16ab9e931d11.png)\n\nThe solution of the system corresponds to the point where the two lines intersect. In this case, it is the point $(2,6)$, as shown above. So $x=2$ and $y=6$ form the **solution** to the system. We can write the solution as the ordered pair $(2, 6)$.\n\nWhenever we find a solution to a system of equations, it is a good idea to double-check our result. To check that the solution $x=2,y=6$ is the correct solution, we substitute our solution into *both* of the equations.\n\nLet's check the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= -4 \\\\\n(2) - (6) &= -4 \\\\\n- 4 &= -4✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we check the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - y &= 6 \\\\\n6(2) - 6 &= 6 \\\\\n6 &= 6✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ and $y=6$ satisfied *both* equations, it is indeed the solution to the system of equations." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139667 #edb/ref 1065 8209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139667 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "84e2025e-9a3e-50ad-9b6b-dcc75fb2b7c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139667 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
